On Dec 20, 11:38 am, Kent Paul Dolan <xanth...@well.com> wrote:
    [...]
> [It's frankly astonishing that Stroustrup didn't get
> rid of this mess of non-standardized data sizes in
> the transition from C to C++.]

The non-standardized data sizes don't really cause any problems
that I know of, and are necessary if you want efficient support
on the widest range of platforms.  (There are machines with 36
bit ints, or with 48 bit ints, being sold today.  There are lots
of machines without IEEE floating point being sold today.)

A standardized GUI library, and a standardized version of
curses, would certainly help.  (So would standardized threading
and sockets, but they're not relevant to nethack.)

    [...]
> Personally, though, I think that NetHack-Next(1) should work
> entirely in tiles mode, and the ASCII character set be just
> another alternate set of tiles for those who prefer an ASCII
> display.

That's the approach I would take in Java.  As another poster has
pointed out, however, this does create problems with things like
the NAO server.  Regardless of the language, I would suggest a
separate display engine, interfaced through interfaces, with the
actual display engine using plug-in technology.  Only the
traditional ASCII display engine needs to know about curses.h.
(Is there actually any other way to implement interactive
software?)

    [...]
> Similarly, no matter which set of tiles is in use, ASCII or
> otherwise, it should be possible to do a screen capture into
> plain ASCII text (discarding character colors) for use here in
> rgrn, as well as to do a screen capture of any set of tiles in
> use as a layer into a *.PNG image.

Separate functionality?  Using a different display engine (or a
modified version of the ASCII display engine)?

